@title Runbook: settlement-consumer lag alert
@subtitle Streaming team · Corvid Payments · settlement-consumer group on payments-events

# Alert thresholds

table = Severity,Lag (messages),Duration,Action; Warning,50000,10 minutes,Slack only; Critical,150000,5 minutes,page on-call; Emergency,1000000,any,page on-call and the Streaming lead

# Triage flow

diagram triage title="Consumer lag triage"
triage: alert "Lag alert fires" ext
triage: running "Consumers running?" svc
triage: restart "Restart consumers" svc
triage: skew "Partition skew?" svc
triage: rebal "Rebalance partitions" svc
triage: scale "Scale out consumers" svc
triage: dbchk "Check downstream DB" db
triage: recover "Lag recovering" svc
triage: alert -> running
triage: running -> restart : no
triage: running -> skew : yes
triage: skew -> rebal : yes
triage: skew -> scale : no
triage: restart -> recover
triage: rebal -> recover
triage: scale -> dbchk
triage: dbchk -> recover

# Steps

1. Check the consumer pods in the streaming namespace.
2. Check per-partition lag.
3. If one partition holds more than 40 percent of the lag, treat it as skew.
4. Scale the deployment to at most 24 replicas, because the topic has 24 partitions.

!! Never reset consumer offsets to latest. Unprocessed settlements would be skipped and would have to be reconciled by hand.

If lag is still growing after 30 minutes, escalate to the Streaming lead.
